cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "arqlonghaul: ARQ and hybrid-ARQ goodput on links where the round-trip time dominates"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-06
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Slotted state-machine simulators for stop-and-wait, go-back-N and selective
  repeat, validated against the classical closed-form throughput expressions in
  the regime where those expressions are exact (independent frame errors, large
  windows) to a worst 0.616 per cent relative deviation over 78 cases; window
  sizing against the bandwidth-delay product in frames and bytes, including the
  measured shortfall of the textbook one-bandwidth-delay-product window;
  correlated frame errors from a Gilbert-Elliott channel parameterised so that
  marginal error rate and mean burst length are set independently, and the
  measured misstatement of the independent-error expressions on such a channel;
  type-I chase combining and type-II incremental redundancy over a
  Singleton-bound bounded-distance decoder model, computed by an exact nested
  dynamic program and cross-checked against Monte Carlo, with the
  retransmit-versus-redundancy crossover located and its sensitivity to every
  assumption reported; and a learned per-round redundancy policy with an
  ensemble uncertainty output, benchmarked on three disjoint seed sets against
  an analytic fixed schedule, a tuned fixed schedule and an adaptive heuristic,
  where the tuned fixed schedule wins and that is the published result.
  Validation level 2, research grade. Not flight-qualified, not certified, not
  approved for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - arq
  - hybrid-arq
  - harq
  - incremental-redundancy
  - go-back-n
  - selective-repeat
  - gilbert-elliott
  - bandwidth-delay-product
  - deep-space-communications
references:
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Gilbert
        given-names: "E. N."
    title: "Capacity of a burst-noise channel"
    journal: "The Bell System Technical Journal"
    volume: 39
    issue: 5
    start: 1253
    end: 1265
    year: 1960
    notes: >-
      The two-state burst-error channel used in arqlonghaul.channel. Title and
      publication date were confirmed against the Nokia Bell Labs publication
      page for this paper on 2026-10-06; the volume, issue and page numbers are
      as given in the reference list of arXiv:2005.06921, read the same day.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Elliott
        given-names: "E. O."
    title: "Estimates of error rates for codes on burst-noise channels"
    journal: "The Bell System Technical Journal"
    volume: 42
    issue: 5
    start: 1977
    end: 1997
    year: 1963
    notes: >-
      The extension to a non-zero error probability in the good state, which is
      the form implemented here. Verified the same way as Gilbert 1960.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Lin
        given-names: Shu
      - family-names: Costello
        given-names: "Daniel J."
        name-suffix: Jr.
    title: "Error Control Coding"
    edition: "2nd"
    publisher:
      name: "Prentice Hall"
    year: 2004
    notes: >-
      Source for the ARQ throughput expressions in arqlonghaul.closedform and
      for the bounded-distance decoder block error probability in
      arqlonghaul.harq. No page numbers are quoted because none were verified
      against the printed text in this build.
  - type: book
    authors:
      - family-names: Tanenbaum
        given-names: "Andrew S."
    title: "Computer Networks"
    notes: >-
      The sliding-window efficiency derivations, which agree with the Lin and
      Costello forms. No edition or page numbers are quoted because none were
      verified in this build.
  - type: standard
    title: "TM Synchronization and Channel Coding"
    number: "CCSDS 131.0-B-5"
    institution:
      name: "Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems"
    notes: >-
      Cited as the channel-coding context for space links, and as the source of
      the frame size order of magnitude used in the link presets. The document
      number and title were confirmed on 2026-10-06 against the European
      Cooperation for Space Standardization adoption notice ECSS-E-AS-50-21C
      Rev.2 (5 December 2024), which adopts CCSDS 131.0-B-5 under that exact
      title. This repository does not quote any numerical value from the
      standard.
  - type: standard
    title: "Licklider Transmission Protocol - Specification"
    number: "RFC 5326"
    institution:
      name: "Internet Engineering Task Force"
    notes: >-
      Cited as the long-delay retransmission context. Existence, number and
      title confirmed at rfc-editor.org on 2026-10-06. No numerical value from
      it is used here.
  - type: software
    title: "crcmod"
    version: "1.7"
    notes: >-
      Not a dependency and never imported. Its source distribution file
      python3/crcmod/predefined.py was read on 2026-10-06 to obtain the CRC
      catalogue check values that validation/validate_crc.py checks against.
